<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bidayuh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Land Dayaks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bidayuh are an indigenous people of south-western Sarawak in Malaysian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, living mainly in the hills around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuching">Kuching</a>, and were formerly called Land <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayaks</a>. Traditionally hill-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers living in longhouses, with distinctive round head-houses, they joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> in the revolt of 1836–1841 against Brunei's governors, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-brooke">James Brooke</a> helped to end and which brought him to power in Sarawak.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bidayuh.jpg" alt="Bidayuh" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarawak;_a_native_Land_Dayak_chief._Photograph._Wellcome_V0037472.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
